module gunbc.spark.serving_arm_placement

import std.types { List, NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.fleet_intent_network { operator_host_srv9 }

// WHICH HOST EACH DECLARED SERVING ARM OCCUPIES, AND NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THE ARM.
//
// WHY THIS IS ITS OWN AUTHORITY RATHER THAN A FIELD READ OFF THE ARM. The GLM canary's host lived on
// `glm_canary_arm.host` in gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge, which is the arm's convergence root and
// imports gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection for its candidate vocabulary. Any census of what
// the fleet is spoken for by must read that claim -- and gunbc.spark.host_commitment is imported BY
// serving selection in the follow-up, so a census reaching up into the canary module would close the
// loop selection -> host_commitment -> glm_canary_converge -> selection, which the import graph's one
// law refuses. The placement fact is the part both sides need and the only part, so it sits below
// both and neither module grows a second copy of it.
//
// A CLAIM IS NOT A CAPABILITY AND NOT A LEASE. This row says an arm has been placed on a host by an
// authority that owns that decision. It does not say the arm is currently running, does not measure
// the host, and confers nothing: a consumer that wants to know whether the process is alive must
// observe it, and a consumer that wants exclusive use must acquire one (see the standing divergence
// recorded in gunbc.spark.host_commitment). What it does establish is the one thing an emptiness
// check cannot: that SOMEBODY has already spoken for this machine, so a lane placing new work must
// not read it as free.
//
// RELEASE IS A RECEIPT, NOT AN EDIT, and that is stated here rather than modelled here because the
// vocabulary has no consumer yet. Deleting a row below would make a host claimable again on the
// strength of an edit, which is the absence-as-fact inference the llama.cpp reservation lifecycle in
// gunbc.spark.llama_cpp_rpc_observed exists to refuse -- there, a positive observation mints and only
// a positive release receipt with its named evidence clears. The same shape is owed here. TRIGGER:
// the first lane that actually stops a serving arm and reads its host back -- a stop receipt naming
// the process population, listeners and memory state on that host, exactly as
// LlamaCppRpcReleaseReceipt does -- at which point a claim is discharged by that receipt and by
// nothing else. Until then a row is withdrawn only by the authority that placed it.
type ServingArmHostClaim {
  arm: DeclarationRef
  host: HostIdentity
  basis: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE GLM CANARY, WHICH IS SERVING ON srv9 TODAY.
//
// gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment carries the state this row records: the canary is serving on srv9
// and nothing in this repository launched it through the pair route. That module also records why the
// pair-serving claim on group B was WITHDRAWN while this claim was not -- what was given up was a
// claim on hosts, explicitly not a front door -- so an observer reading only the pair roster sees srv9
// as unclaimed and is wrong. This row is what makes the difference readable from below.
data glm_canary_host_claim: ServingArmHostClaim = ServingArmHostClaim {
  arm: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge", decl_name: "glm_canary_arm"),
  host: operator_host_srv9,
  basis: "SPARK canary, operator 2026-09-08: the GLM 5.3 Flash exl3 canary is placed on srv9 and answers there. gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment derives the group B route head from this arm rather than from the pair roster, precisely because the pair claim on group B was withdrawn and the canary was not" as NonEmptyStr,
}

// THE ROSTER. A serving arm placed on a host appears here; the fleet census reads this list and never
// an individual row, so a second arm is one row and no edit anywhere else.
//
// THERE IS NO `spark_serving_arm_claimed_hosts()` BESIDE IT, and the absence is deliberate. One was
// written -- a `map` from this roster to bare host identities -- imported by the census, and never
// called, because the census consumes the CLAIMS: it needs the arm each host is committed to in order
// to say why, and a list of identities throws exactly that away. A projection with no call site is
// DESIGN section 3c's red state, and the honest fix for one is deletion rather than a consumer
// invented to justify it (found by claude review 64213).
data spark_serving_arm_host_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim> = [
  glm_canary_host_claim,
]
